+# TOON-PATCH 0001 (ToonStudio V12 §5 toon-vello fork track).
+#
+# WHY: vello 0.9 pins `wgpu ^29.0.3`, and wgpu 29 has no way to build a
+# `wgpu::Device` around a `GPUDevice` the page already owns
+# (`mod backend` is private, `backend::webgpu::WebDevice.inner` is
+# `pub(crate)`, and `Device::from_custom` demands a whole custom backend
+# rather than adoption). That forces ToonStudio's WGSL filter kernels and
+# vello onto two separate `GPUDevice`s, so every exchange is a CPU readback
+# (V12 §6.3 L0) — measured at 2.9x the same-device GPU copy at 1024²
+# (`tests/benchmarks/results/gpu-fabric-probe.json`).
+#
+# OFF BY DEFAULT ON PURPOSE: with this feature disabled, every patch hunk in
+# this vendored tree compiles out and the crate is API-identical to upstream
+# 29.0.4. That is build track A (`upstream`) — it is what detects upstream
+# regressions. Track B (`toon`) enables `toon-fabric`.
+#
+# REMOVE WHEN: wgpu upstream ships `Device`/`Queue` adoption of an external
+# WebGPU handle AND vello's wgpu pin reaches that release. wgpu 30.0.0
+# already ships the *export* half of this patch (`Device::as_webgpu`,
+# `Texture::as_webgpu`, `Device::create_texture_from_webgpu_handle`, public
+# `wgpu::webgpu` handle module), so those hunks are a straight backport and
+# die with the pin bump; the *adoption* half
+# (`Device::from_webgpu_handle` / `Queue::from_webgpu_handle`) does not
+# exist upstream at all and is our upstream PR candidate (V12 §5.4).
+toon-fabric = []
